<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#british-overseas-territories">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">British Overseas Territories</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2002">2002</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a> · Also known as British Overseas Territory</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The British Overseas Territories are the fourteen territories under British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> that are not part of the United Kingdom, mostly small islands left from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a>, the Falkland Islands, Montserrat and Pitcairn. The name replaced 'British Dependent Territories' in 2002. They govern themselves internally under a governor appointed by the Crown.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/british-overseas-territories.jpg" alt="British Overseas Territories" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rob984 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:United_Kingdom_(%2Boverseas_territories)_in_the_World_(%2BAntarctica_claims).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
